= Lith. ''bruvis'', eyebrow, = Gr. ὀφρύς, eyebrow, = Pers. ''abrū'' = Zend ''brvat'' = Skt. ''bhrū'', eyebrow; cf. Ir. Gael. ''abhra'', eyelid. Perhaps related to ''brae'', ''bray''<sup>4</sup>, q. v., and ult. to E. ''bridge''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' The prominent ridge over the eye, forming an arch above the orbit.—'''2.''' The arch of hair over the eye; the eyebrow.
{{smaller block|Your inky ''brows'', your black silk hair.
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, iii. 5.}}}}
'''3.''' The forehead.
{{smaller block|Beads of sweat have stood upon thy ''brow''.
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., ii. 3.}}}}
'''4.''' The general expression of the countenance.
{{smaller block|He told them with a maisterly ''Brow'', that by this act he had oblig’d them above what they had deserv’d.
{{right|''Milton'', Eikonoklastes, v.}}
To whom thus Satan, with contemptuous ''brow''.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., iv. 885.}}}}
'''5.''' In ''entom.'', that part of an insect’s head which lies between the clypeus and the vertex, generally just above the antennæ.—'''6.''' The edge of a steep place; the upper portion of a slope: as, “the ''brow'' of the hill,” Luke iv. 29.—'''7.''' In England, a fringe of coppice adjoining the hedge of a field.—'''8.''' In ''coal-mining'', an underground roadway leading to a working-place, driven either to the rise or to the dip. ''Gresley.'' [Leicestershire, Eng.]—'''9.''' ''Naut.'', an old name for an inclined plane of planks from the

